Stop GiNaC::power::to_polynomial() from recursing forever on (x^a)^(-n), i.e. a negative integer
power of a power whose own exponent is symbolic.

The negint branch rebuilds pow(basis_pref, exponent) and recurses into it, justified by the comment
"(A*B)^n will be automagically transformed to A^n*B^n". That holds for a `mul` basis, and for a
`power` basis whose inner exponent is numeric ((x^3)^(-2) evaluates to x^(-6)). It does NOT hold for
a `power` basis with a SYMBOLIC inner exponent: power::eval only contracts (x^a)^b into x^(a*b) when
that is provably valid, since for symbolic a the two sides differ across the branch cut. So
pow((x^a), -2) evaluates back to the very expression to_polynomial was called on, and the recursion
is a self-call with an unchanged argument -- roughly 47000 frames deep here before the stack runs
out, with no diagnostic of any kind.

Recursing only when the rebuilt expression actually differs leaves every terminating case
bit-identical (there t differs from *this by construction) and turns the non-terminating one into
the same opaque-symbol replacement that to_polynomial already applies to any other non-polynomial
subexpression -- including, without this patch, the closely related (x^a)^(1/2).

pyoomph reaches it through unit extraction: collect_base_units() calls collect_common_factors(),
which calls to_polynomial(). A Vignes-interpolated Maxwell-Stefan diffusivity written as
D0_ij^e_ij * D0_ji^e_ji has composition FIELDS in its exponents, and when the mixture viscosity
inside D0 is a sum the unit symbols do not cancel automatically, leaving factors of the form
kilogram^(<field expression>) for collect_common_factors to choke on. See
dev_docs/examples/ginac_to_polynomial_symbolic_exponent.cpp, which reproduces it in five lines with
no pyoomph involved and is what to send upstream.

--- a/ginac/normal.cpp
+++ b/ginac/normal.cpp
@@ -2720,10 +2720,13 @@
 		if (is_exactly_a<mul>(basis_pref) || is_exactly_a<power>(basis_pref)) {
 			// (A*B)^n will be automagically transformed to A^n*B^n
 			ex t = pow(basis_pref, exponent);
-			return t.to_polynomial(repl);
+			// ... but a power with a SYMBOLIC exponent is not, and rebuilding it unchanged
+			// would make this an infinite self-recursion. Fall through to the replacement
+			// below instead, which is what any other non-polynomial basis gets.
+			if (!t.is_equal(*this))
+				return t.to_polynomial(repl);
 		}
-		else
-			return pow(replace_with_symbol(pow(basis, _ex_1), repl), -exponent);
+		return pow(replace_with_symbol(pow(basis, _ex_1), repl), -exponent);
 	} 
 	else
 		return replace_with_symbol(*this, repl);
